.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CLIENT\-C" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The C client binding provides a synchronous, blocking interface to the
bash\-server v2 NDJSON protocol.
It is implemented as \f[CR]libbashclient\f[R], a C99 library with POSIX
dependencies only (no external libraries).
The public API is declared in a single header, \f[CR]bashclient.h\f[R],
and the library is available in both static (\f[CR]libbashclient.a\f[R])
and shared (\f[CR]libbashclient.so\f[R]) forms.
.PP
Unlike the Python, TypeScript, and Java bindings, the C binding is fully
synchronous.
There is no background thread or event loop.
Server\-push messages (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY output) are
delivered through registered function\-pointer callbacks when the caller
invokes \f[CR]bc_poll()\f[R].
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Installation
The library is located at \f[CR]bash\-server/clients/c/\f[R] and uses a
plain Makefile:
.IP
.EX
cd bash\-server/clients/c
make
.EE
.PP
This produces:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]libbashclient.a\f[R] \(en Static library
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]libbashclient.so\f[R] \(en Shared library
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]include/bashclient.h\f[R] \(en Public header
.PP
To link against the library:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Static\f[R]
cc \-o myprogram myprogram.c \-Ibash\-server/clients/c/include \-Lbash\-server/clients/c \-lbashclient

\f[I]# Shared\f[R]
cc \-o myprogram myprogram.c \-Ibash\-server/clients/c/include \-Lbash\-server/clients/c \-lbashclient
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=bash\-server/clients/c ./myprogram
.EE
.PP
Tests are built and run with:
.IP
.EX
make test
.EE
.SH Source structure
.IP
.EX
include/
    bashclient.h     \-\- Public API (opaque handle, all declarations)
src/
    bashclient.c     \-\- Client logic, channel dispatch, polling
    internal.h       \-\- Internal structures (bc_client internals)
    protocol.c       \-\- NDJSON encode/decode, base64
    transport.c      \-\- Unix socket, stdio, fd, Named Pipe transports
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central type is \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R], an opaque handle that
encapsulates the transport connection, internal message buffers,
callback registrations, and per\-channel response state.
.PP
All operations are synchronous and blocking.
When a function like \f[CR]bc_eval()\f[R] is called, it:
.IP "1." 3
Encodes the request as an NDJSON line and writes it to the transport.
.IP "2." 3
Reads lines from the transport until it receives the expected response
message(s) for the target channel.
.IP "3." 3
While reading, any server\-push messages encountered are buffered
internally.
.IP "4." 3
Returns the result through an output parameter.
.PP
Server\-push messages (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY output/exit)
accumulate in an internal buffer.
The caller must periodically call \f[CR]bc_poll()\f[R] to dispatch them
to registered callbacks.
Alternatively, push messages that arrive while waiting for a
request/response are dispatched immediately if callbacks are registered.
.SH Connection
Four functions create a \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] handle:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* Unix domain socket (default) */\f[R]
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);

\f[I]/* Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode) */\f[R]
const char *argv[] = {\(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq, NULL};
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect_stdio(argv);

\f[I]/* Inherited file descriptor */\f[R]
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect_fd(3);

\f[I]/* Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin) */\f[R]
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect_named_pipe(\(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq);
.EE
.PP
All connect functions return \f[CR]NULL\f[R] on error; check
\f[CR]errno\f[R] for the failure reason.
.SH Lifecycle
.IP
.EX
bc_client_t *c = bc_connect(socket_path);
\f[B]if\f[R] (!c) {
    perror(\(dqbc_connect\(dq);
    exit(1);
}

int rc = bc_auth(c, token);
\f[B]if\f[R] (rc != BC_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, \(dqauth failed: %s\(rsn\(dq, bc_error(c));
    bc_close(c);
    exit(1);
}

\f[I]/* ... use client ... */\f[R]

bc_close(c);  \f[I]/* sends disconnect, frees all resources */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]bc_close()\f[R] sends a \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message, closes the
transport, and frees all internal state including the
\f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] handle itself.
After \f[CR]bc_close()\f[R], the pointer is invalid.
