.EE
.SH Memory management
The C binding follows a consistent memory ownership convention:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Strings returned by the API\f[R] (e.g., in result structs) are
allocated by the library and must be freed by the caller via
\f[CR]bc_free()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Struct contents\f[R] are freed by type\-specific free functions
(\f[CR]bc_eval_result_free()\f[R], \f[CR]bc_var_info_free()\f[R], etc.).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Array results\f[R] (e.g., breakpoint lists) are freed with their
type\-specific free function plus a count parameter.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* String in struct \-\- freed by type\-specific function */\f[R]
bc_eval_result_t result;
bc_eval(c, \(dqecho hello\(dq, &result);
printf(\(dq%s\(dq, result.stdout_data);
bc_eval_result_free(&result);

\f[I]/* String returned directly \-\- freed by bc_free */\f[R]
char *json;
bc_state_inspect(c, \(dqvariables\(dq, &json);
printf(\(dq%s\(rsn\(dq, json);
bc_free(json);

\f[I]/* Array \-\- freed with count */\f[R]
bc_breakpoint_t *bps;
int count;
bc_debug_list_breakpoints(c, &bps, &count);
bc_breakpoint_list_free(bps, count);
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Never use \f[CB]free()\f[B] directly\f[R] on pointers returned by
the library.
Always use \f[CR]bc_free()\f[R] or the appropriate type\-specific free
function.
.SH Thread safety
The C binding is \f[B]NOT thread\-safe\f[R].
Use one \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] per thread.
Do not share a client handle between threads.
Do not call any \f[CR]bc_*\f[R] function from a signal handler.
.SH CONTROL channel
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* Authenticate */\f[R]
int rc = bc_auth(c, token);

\f[I]/* Ping */\f[R]
rc = bc_ping(c);
.EE
